I'm curious whether the state machines will be rooted by Task.s_currentActiveTasks when run under a debugger. That's a problem I noticed when writing the CriticalErrorLoggedIfApplicationDoesntComplete test.

https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/4a7bf75e89e3dfe7264a641ed041f50788d28d20/src/Servers/Kestrel/test/InMemory.FunctionalTests/HttpConnectionManagerTests.cs#L24-L29

@davidfowl thinks this won't be an issue since ProcessDataWrites is ValueTask-returning, but it's probably worth double checking. The improved simplicity may not be worth it if it leads to a memory leak with a debugger attached.

```cs
static void Main(string[] args)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
    {
        _ = DoAThingValueTask();
        _ = DoAThingTask();
    }
    var t = Task.CompletedTask;
    Console.WriteLine("Hello World!");
    Console.ReadKey();
}

private static async ValueTask<ReadResult> DoAThingValueTask()
{
    Pipe p = new Pipe();
    return await p.Reader.ReadAsync();
}

private static async Task<ReadResult> DoAThingTask()
{
    Pipe p = new Pipe();
    return await p.Reader.ReadAsync();
}
```

